SHARED ART DIRECTION:
{
  "style_bible": "A restrained symbolic psychological illustration language for an adult Japanese audience: monochrome black charcoal, ink wash, off-white paper texture, and a single controlled gold accent #FFD700 reserved for thresholds, handles, or tiny moments of possible agency. Use high-contrast compositions, thick imperfect brush outlines, sparse hand-drawn texture, broad negative space, and soft atmospheric ink fades rather than hard panels or decorative backgrounds. Every image is text-free: no captions, labels, signs, screens, book covers, diagrams, or readable marks. Favor one dominant psychological symbol and one readable human action per frame. Visual metaphors may express habit, learned avoidance, control, shame, inner conflict, and symbolic resistance, but must remain editorial illustrations rather than evidence or diagnosis. Maintain continuity through recurring circular paths, half-open thresholds, backward-pulling shadows, repeated objects in altered positions, and small gold points of potential movement. Avoid photorealism, detailed anime styling, celebrity resemblance, medical imagery, literal brain graphics, busy interiors, excessive color, multiple focal subjects, and melodramatic horror. Camera language is mostly frontal or slightly elevated, with occasional close crops at the exact moment before action. The overall rhythm alternates between recognizable everyday vignettes and spacious symbolic tableaux, always returning to the half-open doorway motif.",
  "character_bible": "The recurring fictional chalk-line figure is an anonymous adult Japanese silhouette with a round unfeatured head, slim neutral proportions, simple off-white chalk lines, restrained dot eyes only when necessary, and black charcoal clothing blocks. Keep the same head silhouette, body proportions, line weight, and charcoal/off-white treatment in every appearance. The figure has no hairstyle, facial identity, age-specific details, accessories, or distinctive personal features. Body language carries the psychology: shoulders leaning toward a possibility while feet remain anchored, a hand hovering before action, a torso folding back toward familiarity, or a posture divided between forward intention and backward protection. The figure's shadow is not a second character; it is an oversized black symbolic extension of the same figure, capable of gripping a wrist, repeating a path, forming a wall, or closing around familiar objects. Use only one recurring figure unless a scene explicitly requires a faint crowd of identical anonymous silhouettes to represent social conditioning; never introduce a named or individualized character. Gold may touch the door handle, threshold, or a tiny point of agency, but never the face or clothing.",
  "environment_bible": "Environments are minimal psychological spaces built from off-white paper, charcoal fields, ink gradients, and a few concrete props when needed for behavioral recognition. Use a quiet adult workspace, a narrow hallway, a desk edge, a blank wall, a phone-like rectangular glow without any visible interface, or an abstract threshold; never show readable text. Familiarity is represented by circular floor marks, repeated shelves, looping corridors, stacked dark shapes, or objects returning to the same position. New possibility is represented by a narrow pale opening, a thin gold threshold glow, or a clean area of paper beyond an obstruction. The half-open doorway is the recurring environmental anchor, but vary its framing and function so it does not become a literal repeated scene: it may appear as a door, a gap in a wall, a narrow passage, or a bright interruption in a charcoal boundary. Keep backgrounds quiet enough for the central action to read at small size. Gold is strictly limited to one or two small accents per frame; all other elements remain black, charcoal, off-white, and gray ink. No in-image language of any kind, including Japanese, Vietnamese, English, pseudo-writing, interface symbols, or decorative typography.",
  "opening_visual_contract": {
    "thumbnail_scene": "The thumbnail shows the recurring chalk-line figure at a half-open doorway, one hand on a controlled-gold handle, while a huge black shadow grips the wrist and pulls backward. The composition is a singular conflict between entering a new possibility and being returned to familiar darkness.",
    "first_frame_scene": "Begin with an indirect visual payoff rather than repeating the thumbnail: an extreme close-up of the recurring figure's off-white chalk hand hovering a few centimeters above a narrow gold-lit threshold line on the floor. The doorway itself is initially outside the frame. Behind the hand, a dense charcoal shadow forms a circular loop, suggesting the familiar route without yet showing the wrist being grabbed. Off-white paper grain, black ink field, one tiny gold accent, no text.",
    "first_15s_visuals": [
      "0-3 seconds: Hold on the hovering hand and the thin gold threshold line. The hand trembles almost imperceptibly while the circular shadow behind it remains perfectly still, creating immediate tension before action.",
      "3-6 seconds: Slowly widen the frame to reveal the figure's round unfeatured head and slim body leaning forward, while both feet remain planted inside a dark circular brush mark. The new opening is suggested only by pale light entering from the edge of frame.",
      "6-9 seconds: The shadow lengthens across the floor and gently loops around one ankle, not violently, making the resistance feel familiar and protective rather than monstrous. Keep the figure unchanged and text-free.",
      "9-12 seconds: Cut to a quiet behavioral vignette of the same figure opening a blank device-like rectangle or document surface, then turning toward a small secondary task represented by neatly aligned charcoal shapes. No interface, icons, letters, or readable marks.",
      "12-15 seconds: Match-cut the aligned charcoal shapes into the bars of a half-open doorway. The figure's hand reaches toward the gold handle from a new angle, visually connecting everyday diversion to the symbolic threshold and paying off the thumbnail without duplicating its composition."
    ]
  }
}
