{
  "style_bible": "A symbolic long-form psychological visual language built from high-contrast black ink, charcoal silhouettes, sparse hand-drawn brush texture, off-white paper, and exactly one restrained gold #FFD700 accent when the old key appears. Use editorial illustration rather than literal evidence: quiet Jungian depth, strong negative space, tactile paper grain, soft charcoal fades, and simple readable compositions. Every frame is text-free: no Japanese characters, letters, labels, signs, screens, captions, or typography inside the image. Favor one dominant psychological symbol, one clear human tension, and restrained movement. Use recurring visual motifs of old keys, absent keyholes, open thresholds, black ink bands, shadows, and separated fragments gradually becoming integrated. Avoid photorealism, glossy rendering, cinematic realism, medical imagery, diagnostic symbolism, horror, clutter, literal trauma depiction, and decorative color. Reuse compositions through gradual changes in posture, distance, shadow density, and the relationship between the figure and the threshold. Symbolic scenes are editorial illustrations and must never be presented visually as research evidence.",
