SHARED ART DIRECTION:
{
  "style_bible": "A unified symbolic editorial-illustration language for a Japanese adult psychology video: high-contrast black charcoal and ink on an off-white paper field, restrained hand-drawn brush texture, thick imperfect outlines, sparse composition, and one controlled gold #FFD700 accent used only for the recovering boundary line. Use quiet negative space, soft charcoal fades, and visual metaphors rather than literal explanations. The recurring motif is a thin boundary line on paper: initially blurred, erased, crossed, or hidden, then gradually restored and made visible. Emotional tension should be shown through posture, shadow shape, distance, pressure, and the condition of the boundary—not through facial detail or dramatic effects. Maintain a calm, heavy, reflective mood with immediate readability at television-grid size. All visuals remain text-free: no Japanese lettering, no Latin lettering, no labels, no signs, no screens, no captions, no logos, and no readable marks. Never use photorealism, detailed anime styling, celebrity resemblance, diagnosis imagery, gore, medical symbols, or literal claims presented as visual evidence. Symbolic scenes are editorial illustrations only. Preserve visual continuity through the same charcoal/off-white treatment, the same line weight, the same restrained gold accent, and recurring compositions that move from erased boundaries toward conscious recognition and self-integration.",
  "character_bible": "The recurring fictional chalk-line figure is an anonymous adult Japanese silhouette with an unchanged round unfeatured head, slim neutral proportions, simple off-white chalk contour lines, and black charcoal clothing blocks. The face contains no realistic features; use only restrained dot eyes when absolutely necessary, with no expressive cartoon face. Keep the exact head silhouette, body proportions, line weight, clothing abstraction, and charcoal/off-white treatment consistent across every appearance. The figure is not a named person, patient, diagnosis, or biographical subject. Emotional states are communicated through small posture changes: a polite forward tilt for appeasement, rounded shoulders for exhaustion, a hand hovering near the mouth for self-censorship, a delayed turn toward the boundary for recognition, and a steadier upright stance when the boundary becomes visible. The shadow is always the figure's own psychological silhouette, never a second person or independent entity. It may stretch, bend, preserve, or reveal the erased boundary, but must remain visually connected to the figure. Use no additional protagonists unless required by a vignette; if another anonymous silhouette appears, keep it minimal, faceless, secondary, and stylistically identical.",
  "environment_bible": "Use a nearly abstract off-white paper world with sparse charcoal brush texture and no identifiable real-world location unless a simple vignette requires one. Floors, walls, tables, rooms, streets, and social spaces should be reduced to a few ink planes or horizon marks, never rendered as detailed backgrounds. The thin boundary line is the primary environmental object: a fragile horizontal or curved chalk mark beneath, between, or around the figure. At first it may be smudged, interrupted, rubbed away, or hidden beneath charcoal dust. Over the course of the video it should gradually reappear as a clean, narrow line, with one controlled gold #FFD700 fragment marking the point of recognition or restored self-protection. Keep gold rare and localized; use no red, neon, uncontrolled color, glowing effects, or decorative gradients. Avoid desks, phones, notebooks, calendars, books, documents, screens, signage, and any object that would require text. When illustrating behavioral recognition, show simple symbolic actions such as a figure smiling while stepping back from a crossed line, raising a hand without speaking, replaying a conversation through looping charcoal strokes, or carrying a dark weight attached to the erased boundary. Maintain a single visual hierarchy: figure first, boundary second, shadow third, environment last.",
  "opening_visual_contract": {
    "thumbnail_scene": "The thumbnail establishes a forced smile, a hand erasing a thin boundary beneath the figure, and the figure's own oversized shadow preserving the erased line. It uses a single controlled gold fragment as the only color accent and leaves the emotional contradiction immediately readable without literal text.",
    "first_frame_scene": "Open on an extreme quiet close view of the off-white paper floor. A thin charcoal boundary line runs across the lower third, already partially rubbed away. The recurring chalk-line figure enters only as a cropped lower body and hand at the edge of frame, frozen in the act of wiping the line. The figure's shadow falls behind the erased area, but its outline still traces the missing boundary. No face, text, or explanatory object is visible. A tiny gold fragment remains at the exact place where the line has not yet disappeared, creating a visual match to the thumbnail without repeating its full composition.",
    "first_15s_visuals": [
      "0-3 seconds: Hold on the smudged paper surface and the damaged boundary line. Fine charcoal dust drifts minimally as the hand withdraws, leaving the line weaker but not gone.",
      "3-6 seconds: The camera slowly pulls back to reveal the recurring figure's slim silhouette and restrained smile, while the figure looks outward rather than downward, unaware of what the hand has erased.",
      "6-9 seconds: The figure's own shadow lengthens behind them and quietly redraws the missing section of the boundary, showing that the denied feeling has not disappeared.",
      "9-12 seconds: The figure takes one small step across the blurred line. The shadow remains on the original side, creating a visual split within one silhouette rather than introducing a second character.",
      "12-15 seconds: The camera settles into a clean three-quarter composition: the smiling figure in front, the preserved shadow-line behind, and one narrow gold fragment at the pressure point. The image holds long enough for the viewer to recognize the central contradiction before the narrative expands."
    ]
  }
}
