  "environment_bible": "All environments are symbolic editorial spaces built on an off-white paper field with sparse charcoal brush texture. Prefer empty paper planes, shallow floor-like surfaces, soft ink horizons, circular pools of shadow, and thin hand-drawn boundary lines over realistic rooms. When a vignette requires an interpersonal setting, reduce it to abstract silhouettes, a shared ground line, a chair-like block, or a doorway-shaped charcoal frame; never use readable signage, documents, phones, computer screens, books, notes, labels, or other text-bearing objects. The boundary line is the central environmental element and may appear beneath the character's feet, between two silhouettes, around a small protected area, or as a faint line being rubbed away. Early environments should feel spacious but emotionally constricted, with the boundary barely visible and charcoal pressure accumulating around it. Middle environments may show repeated erased marks, overlapping shadow strokes, narrow corridors of paper, or a figure standing outside their own faint boundary. Later environments should show the line becoming continuous, clean, and consciously redrawn without turning the scene into a triumphant spectacle. Use only black, charcoal gray, off-white, and rare gold #FFD700. Gold must be a thin accent, never a full object, glow, wash, or decorative color. Camera language is slow and deliberate: restrained push-ins, top-down symbolic compositions, lateral tracking along a boundary, and match cuts between erased and restored lines. Keep compositions readable at television size and reuse the same paper texture and visual geometry throughout.",
  "opening_visual_contract": {
    "thumbnail_scene": "The thumbnail establishes the central contradiction: the recurring chalk-line figure smiles while unconsciously wiping away a thin boundary line, and the attached oversized shadow preserves the line behind them. The image is a single frontal symbolic tableau with a small controlled gold fragment at the partially erased boundary. No text is rendered inside the image; any headline is added externally during thumbnail compositing.",
    "first_frame_scene": "Begin on an extreme close-up of the same thin boundary line on off-white paper. The line is partially erased, leaving charcoal dust and one narrow gold fragment. At the edge of frame, the recurring figure's simple chalk hand rests beside the damaged line, while the attached shadow falls across the paper in the same direction as the thumbnail shadow. The forced smile and full body are not shown yet, creating a visual question without copying the thumbnail literally.",
    "first_15s_visuals": [
      "0-3 seconds: Hold on the nearly erased boundary line and drifting charcoal dust. The camera makes a very slow inward movement; no text, symbols, or readable marks appear.",
      "3-6 seconds: The figure's hand enters from above and lightly brushes the remaining line away, while a narrow gold fragment stays visible beneath the dust. The action is gentle and automatic, not violent.",
      "6-9 seconds: Match-cut from the disappearing line to the figure's lower body standing on the paper. The figure's posture is polite and still, while the attached shadow remains bent around the boundary that the visible body has erased.",
      "9-12 seconds: Slowly reveal the upper body and restrained forced smile. Keep the face featureless except for minimal dot eyes if needed; the emotional contradiction must come from the smile, lowered shoulders, and shadow.",
