  "style_bible": "A restrained symbolic psychological editorial style based on high-contrast black ink, charcoal, off-white paper, and very limited gold #FFD700 accents. Every image is text-free: no Japanese lettering, captions, signs, labels, screens, or typography. Use one dominant symbolic conflict per frame, with sparse compositions, thick hand-drawn outlines, chalk-like boundary marks, soft charcoal fades, and negative space for narration. The visual language should feel introspective rather than dramatic: quiet pressure, suppressed emotion, delayed recognition, and gradual self-recovery. The recurring motif is a thin boundary line on paper: initially blurred, erased, crossed, or hidden; later restored with a clearer hand-drawn stroke. Use shadows as psychological silhouettes attached to the character, never as separate people, supernatural beings, diagnoses, or literal entities. Gold appears only in small controlled fragments at moments of recognition, boundary awareness, or integration. Avoid photorealism, detailed anime styling, realistic facial features, celebrity resemblance, colorful environments, excessive props, readable text, and explanatory infographic layouts. Maintain visual continuity through the same paper texture, charcoal palette, line weight, figure proportions, shadow behavior, and restrained camera movement. Symbolic scenes are editorial illustrations and must not visually imply research evidence or a clinical diagnosis.",
  "character_bible": "The recurring fictional chalk-line figure is an anonymous adult Japanese silhouette with a simple round unfeatured head, slim neutral proportions, black charcoal clothing blocks, off-white chalk contour lines, and restrained dot eyes only when needed. Preserve the exact head silhouette, body proportions, line weight, and charcoal/off-white treatment from the thumbnail. The figure has no identifiable age, hairstyle, facial detail, or individual biography. Emotional states are shown through posture, hand position, distance from the boundary line, and the relationship between the body and its attached shadow rather than through exaggerated facial expressions. The figure may smile faintly, lower the head, fold the arms, hesitate before a line, or place one hand over the mouth, but must remain understated. The shadow is always the figure's own psychological silhouette: oversized, distorted, delayed, bent, or partially hidden, but visibly connected to the figure by shared contour or grounding. It must never become a second character. Clothing remains charcoal black and visually simple across scenes. When the figure acknowledges anger or restores a boundary, the pose becomes more upright and the line beneath or beside them becomes clearer; the figure does not transform into a heroic or aggressive persona.",
  "environment_bible": "All environments are symbolic editorial spaces built on an off-white paper field with sparse charcoal brush texture. Prefer empty paper planes, shallow floor-like surfaces, soft ink horizons, circular pools of shadow, and thin hand-drawn boundary lines over realistic rooms. When a vignette requires an interpersonal setting, reduce it to abstract silhouettes, a shared ground line, a chair-like block, or a doorway-shaped charcoal frame; never use readable signage, documents, phones, computer screens, books, notes, labels, or other text-bearing objects. The boundary line is the central environmental element and may appear beneath the character's feet, between two silhouettes, around a small protected area, or as a faint line being rubbed away. Early environments should feel spacious but emotionally constricted, with the boundary barely visible and charcoal pressure accumulating around it. Middle environments may show repeated erased marks, overlapping shadow strokes, narrow corridors of paper, or a figure standing outside their own faint boundary. Later environments should show the line becoming continuous, clean, and consciously redrawn without turning the scene into a triumphant spectacle. Use only black, charcoal gray, off-white, and rare gold #FFD700. Gold must be a thin accent, never a full object, glow, wash, or decorative color. Camera language is slow and deliberate: restrained push-ins, top-down symbolic compositions, lateral tracking along a boundary, and match cuts between erased and restored lines. Keep compositions readable at television size and reuse the same paper texture and visual geometry throughout."
}
